+# Riverton Late-Shift Mobility Pilot
+
+## Decision
+
+**Audience:** Riverton City Council Transportation Committee
+
+**Recommendation:** Adopt a six-month, fare-free, on-demand shuttle pilot.
+
+**Recommended service area:** Northpoint only
+
+**Service hours:** 10:00 p.m.–3:00 a.m.
+
+**Fleet and booking:** Two wheelchair-accessible vehicles; booking by app or phone.
+
+The committee should authorize this focused pilot because Northpoint has the packet's clearest observed late-shift access gap, while the on-demand design is projected to reach riders more flexibly than a fixed route. This is a testable recommendation, not a claim that the service has already succeeded. The underlying need and preference findings are observed but limited; costs, waits, and ridership from the options analysis are projections. A six-month pilot would let the city test those assumptions before considering any longer-term service decision. [Service Gap Scan] [Options Memo]
+
+## Evidence
+
+The service-gap scan estimates that 720 Northpoint workers finish between 10:00 p.m. and 2:00 a.m.; just 41% of 120 sampled late-shift endpoints had a scheduled trip home within 45 minutes. In a convenience sample of 88 Northpoint workers, respondents attributed an average 2.1 missed or shortened shifts per month to transportation problems. These findings identify a serious access problem, but they show association rather than causation. [Service Gap Scan]
+
+Community input supports the service model and its access features. Among 214 voluntary survey respondents, 62% preferred an on-demand shuttle and 24% preferred a fixed-route extension. Of 19 respondents identifying a mobility disability, 14 needed a wheelchair-accessible vehicle and 11 needed phone booking; these groups overlap. The reported 71% likely-use figure was conditional on fare-free service. [Community Survey]
+
+For Northpoint, Option A is projected to cost $286,000 over six months, compared with a projected $410,000 for the fixed-route extension. The shuttle's month 6 ridership is projected at 1,050 trips and its median pickup wait at 18 minutes; the fixed route is projected at 780 trips and would put only 52% of sampled endpoints within one-quarter mile of a stop. These are planning projections, not measured results. [Options Memo]
+
+The evidence remains uncertain. The worker study used a convenience sample through five employers and does not establish that transportation caused shift disruption. The community survey was voluntary and nonprobability, and neither service option has operated in Riverton. The committee should therefore treat the figures as a rationale for a bounded test and evaluate actual performance. [Service Gap Scan] [Community Survey] [Options Memo]
+
+## Pilot Design
+
+The city should operate two wheelchair-accessible vehicles within Northpoint from 10:00 p.m. to 3:00 a.m. for six months. Riders should pay no fare and should be able to book either in the app or through a staffed phone line. Dispatch should retain the modeled two-vehicle plan while reviewing demand by hour every week. The city research team should use the packet's 2.1-shift worker figure as the pre-launch comparison, keep individual panel responses separate from trip operations data, and rely on aggregate vendor reporting to the committee. This preserves access for riders who cannot use a smartphone and aligns implementation with needs in the packet. [Community Survey] [Options Memo]
+
+## Measures
+
+The dashboard should report the same definitions every month. Trips and wait time begin with the new service; the shift-disruption baseline comes from the Northpoint worker sample. Month 6 targets come from the Northpoint-only planning case and must be labeled as targets rather than guarantees. [Service Gap Scan] [Options Memo]
+
+| Measure | Baseline | Month 6 target |
+|---|---:|---:|
+| Completed passenger trips per month | 0 | At least 1,000 |
+| Median pickup wait | Not applicable | 20 minutes or less |
+| Missed or shortened shifts per worker per month | 2.1 | 1.6 or fewer |
+
+Results should be compared with the pre-launch panel without presenting a simple before-and-after change as proof of causality.
+
+## Risks and Mitigations
+
+Peak demand could exceed two-vehicle capacity and push the pickup wait above target. Dispatch should review hourly demand weekly and reposition the two vehicles before adding service. An app outage or low digital access could suppress use; maintaining the staffed phone line provides an immediate alternative. Finally, detailed trip and survey records pose privacy and interpretation risks. The vendor should give the committee only aggregated trip and wait data, while the research team stores panel responses separately and reports the sampling and causal limitations with the outcomes. [Options Memo]
+
